  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=-1$ gives a hyperbola\n- $c=0$ gives the $y$-axis, excluding the origin\n- $c=1$ gives the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin",
  "worked_solution": "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & =c \\\\ y & =cx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is the line $y=-x,$ excluding the origin. So, I is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin. So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
